{"name":"Mammary Red","hex":"#b00b1e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mammary","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to the mamma or breast (of a woman or a female animal)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of mamma (“a milk-secreting organ of a woman or a female animal”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mammary"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mammary gland","blocks":[{"text":"A mammary gland is an exocrine gland that produces milk in mammals, including humans. Mammals get their name from the Latin word mamma, \"breast\". The mammary glands are arranged in organs such as the breasts in primates, the udder in ruminants, and the dugs of other animals to feed young offspring. Lactorrhea, the occasional production of milk by the glands, can occur in any mammal, but in most mammals, lactation, the production of enough milk for nursing, occurs only in phenotypic females who have gestated in recent months or years. It is directed by hormonal guidance from sex steroids. In a few mammalian species, male lactation can occur. With humans, male lactation can occur only under specific circumstances."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Breast_anatomy_normal_scheme.png/330px-Breast_anatomy_normal_scheme.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mammary_gland","wikidata":"Q189961"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Normal_breast_histology.png/500px-Normal_breast_histology.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Normal_breast_histology.png/1280px-Normal_breast_histology.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Normal_breast_histology.png/500px-Normal_breast_histology.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Normal histology of the breast","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANormal_breast_histology.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Glandula_mammaria.jpg/500px-Glandula_mammaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Glandula_mammaria.jpg/1280px-Glandula_mammaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Glandula_mammaria.jpg/500px-Glandula_mamma